  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to forecast the probability that by each of the given deadlines (2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, 2050), a correct and complete solution to at least one of the Millennium Prize Problems (excluding Poincar\u00e9) will have been publicly available, with AI contributing a new mathematical argument or proof step. \n\nFirst, I should recall what the Millennium Prize Problems are. The six remaining ones are Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture, Hodge conjecture, Navier-Stokes existence and smoothness, P vs NP, Riemann Hypothesis, and Yang-Mills existence and mass gap. These are all unsolved as of 2025 and are considered extremely challenging in mathematics.\n\nThe event requires a correct complete solution to at least one of these, with AI contributing a new argument or proof step. So, even if there's a correct solution without AI, it doesn't count. Also, the solution must meet the original Clay Institute formulation, and correctness can be validated later, not necessarily validated by the deadline. So the focus is on the solution being public and including an AI contribution.\n\nNow, considering the timelines. Let's break down the deadlines:\n\n- 2026 (next year from 2025)\n- 2030 (5 years from now)\n- 2035 (10 years)\n- 2040 (15 years)\n- 2050 (25 years)\n\nFirst, for 2026: Is it likely that within a year, such a problem will be solved with AI's help? Well, even without AI, solving these problems is a huge task. For example, the Riemann Hypothesis has been open for over a century. AI's role in mathematical research is growing, but in 2025, AI systems like me are more about assisting in areas like data analysis, generating conjectures, or verifying proofs, but not likely to produce a complete proof of such a complex problem. So the chance by 2026 is probably very low. Maybe 0.01 or something. But perhaps over the next five years?\n\nFor 2030: Five years is a bit more time. Some mathematicians might start using AI as a collaborator more effectively. For example, AI could help in identifying patterns, automating proof steps for subproblems, or even suggesting new approaches that humans haven't considered. However, these problems are deep and would require more than just pattern recognition. The probability might be low but higher than 2026. Maybe 0.1?\n\nBy 2035, 10 years from now. By this time, AI could have advanced significantly. Especially if there are breakthroughs in AI's ability to understand and generate mathematical proofs. If AI can work alongside humans in a substantive way, like generating novel lemmas or theorems, then the probability might increase. Maybe 0.2?\n\n2040 (15 years): More time for AI to evolve. If AI systems become super-human in certain aspects of mathematical research, perhaps they can contribute key insights. However, even with advanced AI, solving these problems is not guaranteed. Probability could be around 0.3?\n\n2050 (25 years): Significantly more time. By then, AI might be integrated into most mathematical research, and the chance that a major problem is solved with AI input is higher. Maybe 0.5 or so. \n\nBut I have to account for the fact that some of these problems might take centuries even with AI. So there's a possibility the event never occurs. Also, the probabilities must be non-decreasing. Let me adjust:\n\n2026: 0.01\n\n2030: 0.05 (but maybe too low? Maybe 0.05 is too low. Maybe possibilities for breakthrough in 5 years are more. Maybe 0.02? Wait, original thought for 2026 is 0.01. For 2030, maybe 0.05? Let's see.)\n\nWait, but 2030 is 5 years after 2025. If there's very little chance in 4 years (2026), then 5 years might be 0.05. But maybe the curve is non-linear. Maybe 2026: 0.01, 2030: 0.1, 2035: 0.2, 2040: 0.3, 2050: 0.5? But is that plausible? \n\nAnother angle: Open problems often require insights that are unpredictable. Even with AI, it's not a matter of just running a program. So even with AI assistance, the progress might be slow. \n\nAlso, some of these problems are more approachable than others. For example, P vs NP is notorious, but maybe Yang-Mills and Mass Gap has some physical intuition that could be modeled with AI. Navier-Stokes is more applied. Hodge Conjecture is known for being abstract. \n\nI think that the most likely candidate for an AI-assisted solution might be P vs NP, but even that is extremely difficult. Alternatively, maybe one of the more combinatorial or computational problems might fall to AI-driven iterative proof techniques. \n\nAnother consideration: AI could assist in ways that humans can understand and verify. For the proof step to be \"identifiable\", it needs to be something that is part of the solution. So if AI generates a key lemma or a critical step, then it's counted. \n\nGiven that, I need to estimate how likely it is, for each time frame, that such an event happens. \n\nAlso, considering the historical rate of solving these problems. For example, Poincar\u00e9 was solved in 2003, which took more than a decade from when active research started. So for the remaining problems, which are even more intractable, even with AI, the progress might be slow. \n\nSo for the probability estimates:\n\n2026 (December 31 of 2026) is only about 14 months after the forecast date (April 29, 2025), so the time between 2025 and 2026 is about a year. The chance of a breakthrough in that time is extremely low. So maybe 0.01.\n\n2030 is 5 years ahead. Suppose there's a small chance, say 5% or 0.05. But maybe higher? Maybe 0.1? If progress in AI is rapid, like maybe by 2030, AI is capable enough to assist in major proofs, perhaps 0.1.\n\n2035 (10 years from 2025). If by then AI has significantly advanced, maybe 0.2-0.3.\n\n2040 (15 years): Perhaps 0.3-0.4.\n\n2050 (25 years): Maybe 0.5-0.6.\n\nBut also, the user wants non-decreasing probabilities. Each subsequent year's probability must be higher than the previous. Also, must consider the chance that it never happens, which would mean the 2050 probability is 0.5 or lower. \n\nHowever, I need to balance between optimism and realism. Given that even with advanced AI, solving these problems would be monumental, and some might remain unsolved for a long time. \n\nSo perhaps:\n\n2026: 0.01\n\n2030: 0.05\n\n2035: 0.15\n\n2040: 0.30\n\n2050: 0.50\n\nThis is a way that the probabilities increase but don't become too high. \n\nAnother angle: For example, in the history of mathematics, every 5 years, but even with AI, solving a Millennium Prize Problem is like a 1 in 50 chance each year? Unlikely. So maybe for 5 years, maybe 5% (assuming 1% per year), but with uncertainty. \n\nHowever, the problem is that AI could either accelerate progress or not. If AI can do a lot, then the probabilities could go up faster. If not, then very low. \n\nGiven that the problem requires an identifiable AI contribution in a new argument or proof step, and that collaboration is allowed, maybe by 2050, AI is so integrated into research that some problems get solved. \n\nI think the key is to make the probabilities non-decreasing and account for the possibility that it might never happen (so the 2050 probability doesn't have to be 1.0, can be something like 0.5 or 0.6). \n\nSo putting it all together, the probabilities might look like:\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.01,\n  \"2030\": 0.05,\n  \"2035\": 0.15,\n  \"2040\": 0.30,\n  \"2050\": 0.50\n}\n\nThis is a plausible sequence where the chance increases over time as AI becomes more capable, but even by 2050, there's a 50-50 chance. \n\nBut maybe I'm too optimistic. Let's say that by 2050, the chance is 40% instead of 50. Then:\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.01,\n  \"2030\": 0.05,\n  \"2035\": 0.15,\n  \"2040\": 0.25,\n  \"2050\": 0.40\n}\n\nBut maybe even that is optimistic. Alternatively, perhaps 2026 is 0.005, 2030 0.01, etc. But the user said to give the best estimates despite uncertainty. \n\nI think the initial estimate of 0.01, 0.05, 0.15, 0.3, 0.5 is reasonable. However, some sources suggest that AI may not be able to assist in such high-level mathematical creativity in the near term. For example, current AI can help with proof assistants but not yet create profound theorems. \n\nPerhaps even by 2050, the probability is maybe 0.2. So maybe:\n\n2026: 0.01\n\n2030: 0.02\n\n2035: 0.08\n\n2040: 0.15\n\n2050: 0.25\n\nBut this is too low. However, I think the user wants realistic but considering AI's possible roles. Maybe 0.01, 0.05, 0.15, 0.3, 0.5 is acceptable.\n\nAnother way: Consider that each problem has a low annual probability, but with AI, maybe the probability increases. Let's model this as an exponential increase. Suppose that in 2025, the probability of success per year is p. For a correct solution, with AI contributing a new step. Each year p increases as AI improves. However, quantifying that is hard. \n\nAlternatively, think about how other fields progress. For example, protein folding was solved with AI in AlphaFold. That was a big step. But mathematical proofs are different. So maybe AI-assisted proofs will take time. \n\nIn the case of the Robbins conjecture, a computer automated proof was done in 1996. But those are relatively smaller problems. For a major millennium problem, AI might not yet be the main assistant.\n\nSo, considering all this, I'll stick with the first proposed set, adjusting slightly if needed.\n</think>\n\n{\"2026\": 0.01, \"2030\": 0.05, \"2035\": 0.15, \"2040\": 0.3, \"2050\": 0.5}",
